      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{\u2032}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{3}}}=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{\u2032}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{\u2032} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
