      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{2}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{\u2032}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right),$ which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) &=-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{-3}+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{\u2032}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$",
